entry.summary:
After 6 years doing QA automation in Fintech, I got tired of the same cycle:
Test fails in CI
So I built a custom Playwright reporter that does the debugging for you.
Captures the test name, error message, and stack trace
Here's what the output looks like:
🔍 Analyzing failure: "locked out user sees lock message"
The selector [data-test="error"] matched but the assertion
await expect(loginPage.errorMessage).toContainText(
  'Epic sadface: Sorry, this user has been locked out.'
);

Always assert the exact error string shown in the UI, not a partial guess.
📄 Diagnosis saved → test-results/ai-diagnosis.md
AI_PROVIDER=claude
const provider = createProvider();
const diagnosis = await provider.analyzeFailure(
  test.title,
  result.error?.message ?? 'Unknown error'
);

console.log(`\n🔍 Analyzing failure: "${test.title}"\n`);
console.log(diagnosis);

fs.appendFileSync('test-results/ai-diagnosis.md', diagnosis);

}
}
Screenshot attachment in the diagnosis when available
Try it yourself
https://github.com/sechavarriar/playwright-ai-reporter
npm test
https://github.com/sechavarriar/playwright-ai-reporter — feedback and PRs very welcome. Especially curious if anyone has ideas for handling flaky tests differently.

One JavaScript concept that confused me for a long time is the concept of this. At first, it looks simple. But while using inside a function, the behavior changes based on how the function is called.
It works differently in :
Normal function
Object Method
Arrow Function
In a normal function, this usually refers to the global object or undefined in strict mode. 
function test() {
  console.log(this);
}

The output will show window in a non-strict mode and undefined in a strict mode. 
When a function is called as a method of an object, the this keyword refers to the object that is calling the method.
const user = {
  name: "Swarnali",
  Name() {
    console.log(this.name);
  }
};
user.Name();

//Output : Swarnali

In user.Name(), the object (user) before the dot(.) becomes the value of this …
